The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
	case HCI_ACLDATA_PKT:
		hci_skb_pkt_type(skb) = pkt_type;
		hci_recv_frame(vbt->hdev, skb);
```
Reason:
After reading the one-byte `pkt_type` and pulling `skb` by 1, the code does not verify that `skb->len` is at least the minimum HCI header size (e.g., 4 bytes for ACL). A short packet (e.g., only the `pkt_type` byte) results in `skb->len == 0` and is passed to `hci_recv_frame()`, where `hci_dev_classify_pkt_type()` dereferences `hci_acl_hdr(skb)->handle`, reading uninitialized RX-buffer data.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
	size_t min_hdr;
	__u8 pkt_type;
	...
	switch (pkt_type) {
	case HCI_EVENT_PKT:
		min_hdr = sizeof(struct hci_event_hdr);
		break;
	...
	case HCI_ISODATA_PKT:
		min_hdr = sizeof(struct hci_iso_hdr);
		break;
	...
	}
	if (skb->len < min_hdr) {
		bt_dev_err_ratelimited(vbt->hdev,
				       "rx pkt_type 0x%02x payload %u < hdr %zu\n",
				       pkt_type, skb->len, min_hdr);
		kfree_skb(skb);
		return;
	}
	hci_skb_pkt_type(skb) = pkt_type;
	hci_recv_frame(vbt->hdev, skb);
```
Reason:
The fix introduces a `min_hdr` variable set to the correct HCI header size for each packet type. After the switch, it checks if the remaining `skb->len` is smaller than `min_hdr`; if so, it logs the error and drops the packet. This prevents any short packet from reaching `hci_recv_frame()` and avoids the out-of-bounds read of uninitialized data. The calls to `hci_skb_pkt_type()` and `hci_recv_frame()` are moved after the length validation.

